  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "American investor Charlie Munger valued businesses by estimating the cash they could generate and examining the strength of their competitive position. He preferred that close analysis to forecasts about the wider economy. Investing his own money also suited his desire to make decisions without first securing a client’s agreement.\n\nHis support for the Huntington Library addressed a practical obstacle to scholarship: visiting researchers struggled to afford housing near its California collections. A gift shortly before his death funded construction of residences for those fellows. The plans provided thirty-three residences in seven buildings, with a separate communal space for the visiting scholars.",
    "html": "<p>American investor Charlie Munger valued businesses by estimating the cash they could generate and examining the strength of their competitive position. He preferred that close analysis to forecasts about the wider economy. Investing his own money also suited his desire to make decisions without first securing a client’s agreement.</p>\r\n<p>His support for the Huntington Library addressed a practical obstacle to scholarship: visiting researchers struggled to afford housing near its California collections. A gift shortly before his death funded construction of residences for those fellows. The plans provided thirty-three residences in seven buildings, with a separate communal space for the visiting scholars.</p>\r\n",
